WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:01.140
What do you guys think is real?

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.420
[yells] What a transition!

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.420
[laughs]

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.760
The hardest shot in this film-- right here.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:08.520
That shot right there?

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.240
Hi, welcome back to another episode of VFX Artists React

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.260
Today we're gonna show you some really cool VFX

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.040
and a couple not so cool VFX.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.840
Clint's in Ireland so we're joined by Sam today

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.940
Hey, my name's Sam and I'll be your beetus companion

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.660
Anytime you're watching the beetus of a movie

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.160
Just call me up and I'll give you a tip

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.600
I'll explain what's happening

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:29.380
[laughs]

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.100
So we're gonna kick off this video with 
one of the most requested movies

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.160
That we've ever seen in the comment section

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.680
and it also won our Patreon poll:

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.040
Alita: Battle Angel.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.660
So I actually really enjoyed this movie, it was really fun

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.280
Kinda wonky but very fun

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.340
Look at that facial animation

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.800
So, honestly, when this shot came up on-screen,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.140
I literally said out loud, I was like, "wow."

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.480
Cuz it was very realistic

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.640
Even regardless of the big eyes

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.320
It was very real.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.500
Look how dilated the pupils are.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.040
Like, look how much light is going into those eyeballs

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.060
What is the actual like, canonical lore 
between having big eyes?

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.560
Is that because she's like a super advanced cyborg

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.040
and she needs bigger eyes to have more like cones

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.320
so she can see better?

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.820
The reason is anime. The anime.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:19.520
Their eye budget was all the way up

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:20.400
[yells] Their eye budget?

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:22.860
And when they built the rest of the 
robot's body, they're like

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.479
we'll just make everything else small

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:26.909
Just get the child model for everything else.

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:28.220
Is this good or not?

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.660
I'm just looking at it and it looks weird and I'm like,

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:31.700
I guess it's good

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.300
Is it the face of Alita or is it just like everything about it

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.920
There's a disconnect happening, you know

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.540
The problem is is you're taking this crazy, wacky

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:44.880
anime character and then comparing that to everyday humans in every day world and stuff

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.660
and so, of course, it feels super out of place

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.000
despite the fact that the rendering is like top-notch.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.580
I feel like you get over that really quickly 
when you're watching the movie

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.440
after like, the first five or ten minutes you kinda

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.460
just accept internally that she's a CG character

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.000
Because she is still emoting quite a bit 
throughout the whole movie

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.860
and you believe that it's a real performance.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.346
Rosa Salazar, I think is her name

00:02:04.346 --> 00:02:07.580
did a really good job doing all the facial 
performance capture and whatnot

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.860
The eyes are, you know, the window to the soul.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.300
We express a lot of emotion through our eyes

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:16.280
And for how well everybody can 
read emotion through eyes

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.560
Most people don't know explicitly what to do

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.040
to make those emotions come out.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.780
I say Ren, draw me angry eyes.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.220
You might draw me like, you know, slanted brows.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.680
Yep, that's what I would do, just like--

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.420
but if I went Ren take the eyebrows out

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:30.720
now draw me angry eyes

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:31.620
[laughs]

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:35.780
The point I'm making is there's a lot more going on 
than just the direction of your eyebrows

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.800
a lot of it just has to do with where 
your eyelids sit on your iris.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.180
If your eyelids are raised up off your iris at the top

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.480
that can give you a very, like, rage look.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.665
Your eyelids down a little bit at the top of your iris but completely off the bottom

00:02:47.665 --> 00:02:49.580
That gives you like a "are you kidding me?" look

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:53.440
Happy is actually both of your eyelids coming into your iris a little bit

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.280
So it's entirely where those two skin sleeves on your eye

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.303
Sit relative to your pupil and your iris

00:02:59.303 --> 00:03:01.600
and that's where a lot of emotion comes from.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.580
In Alita, her entire character is basically based 
on where her eyelids sit on her eyes

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:07.640
[laughs]

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.740
So that Zapan character, there?

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:10.880
Is probably my favorite.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:12.280
He's got a little chin spike.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:13.920
He's got a little chin spike, you're right!

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.940
Is it still called a soul patch of a robot, or is it--

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.040
It's like a soul bar.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.260
Soul bar?

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:20.820
It's cool seeing all the different mechanics 
inside that guy's neck move around.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.680
That's my favorite part, honestly

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.320
I'm just trying to figure this out though,

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:25.440
Like, that face looks real.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:26.480
His face looks real.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:27.360
You know why, Sam?

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:28.040
Why?

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.240
'Cause it is real.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:30.220
That would make sense.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.660
His face is real and it's his body that's 
completely computer-generated

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.000
Is that little metal soul patch real, or not?

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.300
It is also real.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.960
I love that they have the goatee on him, for real. [laughs]

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:41.480
Yeah, yeah

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:45.420
Like of all the things to put real on him, it's his goatee.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.720
They actually add makeup along the edges of his face

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.200
to actually make it look like it has thickness.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:54.840
I think they actually re-projected the 
photography of his face onto a 3D model

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.300
to get all the right shadows and lighting and whatnot

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:58.900
Oh my god! What a pain in the butt.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.340
Nah, but it looks so good!

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.340
You know, what's interesting is 
when they recorded these scenes

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.900
They had their movie camera recording the main angle

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.080
And they had a bunch of cameras around the set

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:11.860
Recording her as well, that they can then use to triangulate positions and markers on her suit.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:13.640
All these are like, custom sets

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.540
They're--they're very controlled

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.580
basically they're shooting this within a box

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.800
so that way each shot can be 
processed quickly and efficiently

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.320
unlike Chappie for example where it's just like,

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:25.420
hey, let's do guerilla run-and-gun filmmaking,

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.260
and like put a motion capture s--

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:29.460
in quotes, a "motion capture suit" on him

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:32.020
which ends up being completely useless, you know.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.340
What Sam's saying is that you can spend 
more time on set and less time in post

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.480
or more time in post and less time on set.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:39.440
It depends where your time is more valuable

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.680
Is it more valuable ahead of time or after the fact?

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.180
It's worth noting that when you're on set,

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.020
especially a big set like one of these films,

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.320
and you have a hundred people

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.780
all making at least, you know--

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:52.660
300 bucks a day, most of them probably making more.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.220
It's like a single minute

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.680
costs hundreds of dollars.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.080
What was the budget for this movie?

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.660
Two hundred million dollars was the 
budget of Alita: Battle Angel.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:09.640
So talkin' about a big-budget movie,

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.300
this movie had the biggest budget of any movie

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.820
ever in the history of movies.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.260
That's a complete and utter lie.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.200
The budget for this movie was 15 million dollars.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:17.960
Good for them.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:19.800
That's how much a movie should be made for.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:20.300
Yeah.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.080
It forces you into all these, like,

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.480
problem-solving situations where it's like, alright,

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.310
we can't afford to do all of this, but we 
still want to do something like it.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:30.560
Everyone's got a CG person in their movie these days.

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:33.520
I mean, I love how you can actually see straight through her.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:36.040
You definitely believe that you're looking at a robot,

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:37.520
even though it's so human-like.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:38.160
It looks so good.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:38.680
Really good.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:40.240
What do you guys think is real?

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:42.100
Hands and face.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.140
Of that, I think her face is real

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:46.740
and I think the actual chest, even 
though it looks like her costume,

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:48.080
has also been replaced.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.380
So I'm pretty sure everything 
except for her face has been replaced.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:53.420
You're pretty much right. You're spot-on.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:55.540
Well first off I just want to point out the genius

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:58.320
of designing your costume with little black separators

00:05:58.320 --> 00:05:59.980
for what's real and what's not.

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:03.240
Like on the edges of her wrist, she has a little black bracelet cuff--

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:03.840
Okay.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.260
So there's a very easy-to-find spot where 
you can cut off the real and the CG.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.720
It was actually kind of smart, because there's 
a grid pattern on this costume too,

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.300
that allowed them to sneakily motion track all of it.

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:16.420
The face is real, and her feet are real.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.940
Well that's great, because that's the 
point of contact with the real world

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:20.900
and you want to have that be as realistic as possible.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:22.640
Exactly. That was exactly what they were going for

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:26.000
'cause they knew that all the things that interact 
with real life - your feet and your hands

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.780
Those need to be real so they 
weren't trying to replace those.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:31.400
And of course the face, because 
the face is very hard to replicate.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.660
But the rest of it, not so much.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.620
They kept the face real, which is like

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.720
the main workhorse of an actor, right?

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:37.220
Yeah.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:40.340
Is the face. And so they're able to get all of the emotions

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:44.000
of an actual character but in the body of a robot.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.600
You might think like, "oh, yeah."

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:47.580
"The face is real, the hands are real, the feet are real,"

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:48.240
"Easy."

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:48.800
Do you--

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:49.500
Nope. [laughs]

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:51.080
There are some really hard things.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:53.520
There's a tremendous amount of 
object tracking going on here.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.120
It's all hand-animated.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:58.260
The average shot length was 8 seconds for this movie.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:06:58.800
Most movies

00:06:58.800 --> 00:06:59.300
[groans]

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.120
have an average shot length of like one second.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:02.220
Yeah.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.600
So those like 300, 400 robot shots

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:09.100
are all eight times longer than your 
average blockbuster movie shot.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.620
Here's what became a headache for this movie.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:13.400
So, they need to do the reflections.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.000
Now usually, you just track your--your character

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.640
and then you set up, you know, 
a mirror material or whatever,

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:20.440
in your program, and you get a reflection.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:22.520
It's that easy, right? It should be automatic.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.700
They shot on anamorphic lenses

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.280
meaning that there's slight 
distortion to the environment.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:29.500
And, also the set has slight distortion in it

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:33.280
meaning, not only did they have to 
hand track her in the foreground,

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:36.820
they then had to go and hand 
track her again in the reflection.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:37.720
Yeah.
--Ugh.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:40.420
The hardest shot in this film-- right here.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:41.100
That shot right there?

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.240
That shot. This shot is the hardest shot. 
--How is that so hard?

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:45.160
The actual shot in the movie is like 16 seconds long.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:48.640
When humans are sitting still, 
they're actually never sitting still.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:50.880
We're always moving a little bit.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.740
I'm so steady right now. As still as possible.

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:55.160
You're moving all over the place.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:57.020
So Ren has had the most caffeine this morning.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:57.760
[laughs]

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:00.660
All right, let me just hold up some of this 
cool new Tacticool Reloads merch.

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:01.980
Let me see if I can hold this still.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:03.260
See if we can hold this merch still.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:05.460
This-- did we plan this plug? We didn't plan this.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.860
I'm gonna keep this fast. 
We just launched some new merch.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:10.540
It's very high quality stuff it's 
based on Tacticool Reloads

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:12.300
We also have some other great 
shirt designs in our store:

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:14.940
If you like what we do here

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:17.160
it supports us, please go check it out.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:18.300
And so here's the thing.

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:19.580
It's one thing to like--

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:21.180
Somebody's running down a hallway,

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:22.740
which I said was like the easiest thing to track.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:24.540
Lots of big movements.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.200
It's easy to just track things in by hand.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:27.880
Somebody sitting still,

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:31.100
with their hands wiggling like a pixel at a time

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:32.220
in different directions

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:35.900
That's so perceptually hard to see frame 
by frame if you're tracking it by hand,

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:38.320
but in real life when you're seeing it in motion

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.140
if it's not there, it looks completely wrong.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:43.620
So you're saying the challenge was literally 
just the motion tracking of her arms.

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:44.050
Yes.

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:46.300
Not the compositing it or anything else like that.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:47.320
Just the motion tracking.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:49.660
All these little separate pieces that 
you're doing to track on the arms

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.720
Not just the arm, but the wrist, and the hand, and the thumb, and the fingers, and all the little digits on 'em

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.720
That's a great example of a shot 
that looks completely average

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:57.420
[laughs]

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:00.460
And has a disproportionate amount of work put into it.

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:03.120
Pretty great VFX in this, all around.

