Woo wee!

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:57.880
Didn't even track that explosion. [laughs]

00:09:57.880 --> 00:09:59.500
Just place stuff in there and-- [laughs]

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:01.520
Oh my god! It's literally just like--

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:02.400
[still laughing]

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:06.260
This is nephew grade visual effects right now.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:06.880
Whoa.

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:07.900
Oh this is my favorite, like--

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:08.580
Hold on, wait--

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:09.100
Oh, this guy.

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.120
Oh, see that's CG 
--Malebolgia. Look at that mouth.

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:12.120
What?!
--Now that's CG.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:13.800
Okay way back in the day when this movie came out

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:16.420
like Nico and myself were working on the effects

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:17.000
In 10th grade

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:17.840
In 10th Grade.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:21.200
Now I'm watching this, a movie 
that came out almost at the same time.

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:22.540
We could have done this.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:25.200
It's a lot of work, but we could have done this.

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:26.040
We could have made it look like this.

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:30.000
So I had--I had Deus Ex Machina, which is this 
video that Sam and I made in 10th grade

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:32.420
Do you know how long this shot took to render?

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:35.320
Alright, this shot, by the way, took 30 days to render

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:36.280
[yells] Thirty days!

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:37.600
[laughs]

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:38.740
Thirty days!

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:40.500
Look at that

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.840
What a transition! [all yell]

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:44.880
Oh my god

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:46.040
This is so embarrassing, honestly

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:48.320
it's not embarrassing it's like--  [laughs]

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:51.000
Alright get my MP5 with my silencer [laughs]

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:53.500
Takes the silencer off! [laughs]

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:55.280
This is gonna be a loud one!

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:56.020
[all laugh]

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:58.980
He shoots the jet pack at the guy

00:10:58.980 --> 00:10:59.480
What?!

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:00.500
Like, obviously--

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:02.320
I'm just saying we could have done the visual effects.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:04.540
Obviously their choreography is a little bit tighter

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:05.660
[laughs]

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:06.540
Look at that

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:07.980
This is--this is what I'm talking about when--

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:09.240
Yeah, this is straight-up Spawn
--When we're watching Spawn

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.040
and we're saying we could have done it, just look at this

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:13.140
this is like totally like Spawn level.

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:14.960
Oh, he shot my house!

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:15.460
[laughs]

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:16.580
They show it that I got burned

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:17.740
Wait is that a burning face?
--See I'm like Spawn

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:19.760
I got turned into Spawn!

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:21.560
Dude, you're just flyin'.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:22.860
This is some straight-up Spawn stuff right here
--By the way,

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:24.300
This is literally Spawn, the movie.

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:25.460
Look at that. That's pretty cool.

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:26.880
How do you think we did that Matrix spin?

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:28.520
So, it looks like you're either...

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:30.900
Oh, you know what, I--I bet I know.

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:35.680
You are resting on a stool that can spin

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:36.360
and you're just like...

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:39.900
holding your arms and legs out 
and they're just spinning that

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:41.480
and the cameras staying still.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:42.840
Yep. That's exactly what we're doing.
--Is it?!

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:45.320
I'm literally just on a green screen on a stool

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:47.060
and Nico walks up and spins me

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:48.020
You just match the camera--

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:50.380
Track-- we match the camera to 
how fast I'm spinning on the stool

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:52.800
Anyways, Spawn, our movie--
--anyways, nephew VFX

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:53.860
basically the same.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:57.300
but I think ultimately it's basically the exact same thing.
--it's basically the same

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:57.940
[laughs]

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.120
I dunno. I didn't think it was too bad 
for a movie that's 22 years old.

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:04.740
I think it gets away with it 'cause the 
art direction is actually so awesome and crazy.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:05.240
Yeah.

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:19.620
Aw that's-- that's insane.

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:21.770
Dude, like he's disintegrating and it's all like

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:24.580
fluidy and particle-y and then there's a skeleton

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:25.509
and then he's not there at all.

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:27.660
Honestly as a visual effects artist,

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:32.499
that shot has inspired the way I do action visual effects

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:34.720
it-- so profoundly.

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:38.949
I'm not lying, like every single time 
I do like, someone blowin' up in a shot

00:12:38.949 --> 00:12:41.460
I'm always like thinking this shot.

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:42.460
By the way with this clip right now

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:44.220
We had a lot of comments asking for it

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:45.280
if you would like to see a clip,

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:46.240
please leave a comment below.

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:47.649
We read 'em all, they're great inspiration.

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:51.080
So wait, didn't they do something special 
with how they actually film this on set?

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:53.199
Wasn't he wearing like a special like blue suit?

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:53.700
Yeah.

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:54.580
that was emitting light?

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:57.920
So, Dr Manhattan's, you know, giving off blue light.

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:00.320
The thing is if you want to do that like, "fake"

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:02.000
like you want to be the blue light after the fact

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:04.220
it's a lot of work and it's really hard to make it look real

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:06.040
it's easier to have the actual light in the scene.

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:08.889
They had him wear an LED suit and 
he's wrapped in these LEDs

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:11.080
You can see it in that shot where he crushes the tank.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.500
Right there. You can see the blue 
light shining off the guy's lab jacket.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.180
Oh, that's true. Yeah. Okay.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.000
The glow doesn't feel like glow though.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:18.680
It's like a little dark.

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:19.640
It's-- yeah.

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:21.500
I think it's too strong in the blue channel.

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:22.920
I think that's really the issue, honestly.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:29.380
I think when cameras process color 
if you have too strong of just blue,

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:32.460
it doesn't necessarily register as being bright.

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:35.380
We associate brightness with the color white.

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:40.280
Like regular lights have a spectrum where the LEDs 
will just give you a spike in one wavelength

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:42.880
We're just kinda getting like, 
one spike in the blue wavelength,

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:47.020
which, if another material doesn't have something 
in it to reflect that specific wavelength

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:48.740
it's just not gonna reflect anything.

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:51.110
Technologically speaking, we finally got 
to the point where we could actually

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:53.720
rig up LEDs on a suit for somebody to wear,

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:55.500
like, before this point it wasn't 
even technologically possible

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:58.400
So it was a great idea. It's not really a critique

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:01.480
it's just more like it's a painterly 
character in a real life movie

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:04.480
so you can kind of get away with it 
not being a hundred percent real

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:07.360
It's cool seeing them go 
towards a new technological step

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:11.460
today it could be even more strong as in terms of 
visual impact that would happen

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:14.620
Yeah, they've got pretty bright LEDs these days.

